/** @page execution_modes Execution Modes

    @tableofcontents

    @section exec_mode_intro Introduction

    %Qore can execute code with the AST interpreter, the IR interpreter, LLVM
    JIT compilation, adaptive tiered execution, or ahead-of-time compiled
    artifacts. The same Qore IR is shared by the IR interpreter, JIT, and AOT
    compiler.

    @section exec_mode_overview Execution Mode Overview

    |!Mode|!How to select|!Primary use
    |@ref exec_mode_ast "AST"|Default for non-modern code or <tt>--exec-mode=ast</tt>|Legacy code, maximum compatibility, and predictable baseline performance
    |@ref exec_mode_ir "IR"|<tt>--exec-mode=ir</tt>|Predictable bytecode execution for modern code
    |@ref exec_mode_jit "JIT"|<tt>--exec-mode=jit</tt>|Eager native compilation for long-running code
    |@ref exec_mode_tiered "Tiered"|Default for @ref modern "%modern" code or <tt>--exec-mode=tiered</tt>|Adaptive production execution for modern workloads
    |@ref exec_mode_aot "AOT"|<tt>qcc</tt>|Precompiled executables, modules, and objects

    @section exec_mode_ast AST Execution Mode

    AST mode is the traditional tree-walking interpreter. It is the default for
    legacy non-@ref modern "%modern" code and remains available for modern
    programs when selected explicitly.

    @code{.sh}
qore --exec-mode=ast oldstyle.q
    @endcode

    Use AST mode for:

    - legacy \c .q code that intentionally relies on old-style defaults
    - debugging scenarios where AST execution is required
    - short-lived scripts where native compilation would not amortize

    @section exec_mode_ir IR Execution Mode

    IR mode lowers @ref modern "%modern" code to Qore IR and executes it with
    the IR interpreter. This avoids AST dispatch while keeping startup overhead
    lower than native compilation.

    @code{.sh}
qore --exec-mode=ir script.qr
    @endcode

    IR mode is also the semantic baseline for JIT and AOT; native paths must
    preserve IR behavior exactly.

    @section exec_mode_jit JIT Execution Mode

    JIT mode lowers code to Qore IR and then to LLVM IR for native execution.
    Runtime helpers preserve Qore semantics for calls, exceptions, closures,
    lvalues, object access, and other operations that need runtime services.

    @code{.sh}
qore --exec-mode=jit script.qr
    @endcode

    JIT mode is useful for long-running or CPU-intensive programs where native
    compilation cost is amortized.

    Native compilation runs on a dedicated background thread. In explicit JIT
    mode the program's own functions are compiled to native code up front: a
    parse-commit barrier waits for them to finish before execution begins.
    Functions imported from modules are not all compiled at once; they are
    promoted to native code adaptively on first use, so startup is not dominated
    by compiling the entire imported module stack. When a program is destroyed,
    only its own pending background compilations are drained, so unrelated
    programs' compilation is unaffected.

    @subsection jit_debugging Debugging JIT-Compiled Code

    JIT-compiled code registers symbols through the GDB JIT interface. Debuggers
    can see JIT function symbols after code is compiled and registered.

    @code{.sh}
$ gdb --args qore --exec-mode=jit script.qr
(gdb) set breakpoint pending on
(gdb) break __jit_debug_register_code
(gdb) run
(gdb) info functions _toplevel
    @endcode

    When debugger semantics require AST execution, tiered dispatch can force
    affected variants back to AST. JIT-compiled native code additionally emits
    statement-boundary debug events, so single-stepping, breakpoints, and line
    coverage also work for a native frame that is already running when a debugger
    attaches mid-execution. These hooks are emitted only for programs parsed with
    \c PO_ALLOW_DEBUGGER and remain dormant behind a single atomic check until a
    debugger is actually attached, so they impose no cost on normal execution.

    @subsection jit_optimization_level JIT Optimization Level

    Native code generation applies LLVM optimization passes at a configurable
    level before emitting machine code.

    <b>JIT Optimization Levels</b>
    |!Level|!Description
    |\c 0|No LLVM optimization; the fastest to compile and the slowest to run
    |\c 1|Basic optimization
    |\c 2|A middle setting, retained for callers that want it
    |\c 3|Default; most aggressive optimization, matching the \c qcc AOT compiler

    The level applies only to the LLVM passes; %Qore's own IR optimizer runs
    beforehand in every case, so the difference between levels \c 1, \c 2 and
    \c 3 is normally small, while turning optimization off entirely (\c 0) has a
    measurable cost in generated-code speed.  The default matches the \c qcc AOT
    compiler so that a function generates the same code whether it is compiled by
    the JIT or ahead of time.

    The level is resolved when a function is compiled, so changing it affects
    functions compiled afterwards; functions already compiled to native code keep
    the code they were compiled with.

    The level can be set in three ways, in decreasing order of precedence:
    - per %Program, with @ref Qore::Program::setJitOptimizationLevel()
      "Program::setJitOptimizationLevel()" (or its static form, which applies to
      the current %Program), or from C++ with \c QoreProgram::setJitOptimizationLevel()
    - process-wide, from C++ with \c QoreJIT::setJITOptLevel()
    - process-wide, with the \c QORE_JIT_OPT_LEVEL environment variable

    A %Program that sets no level of its own uses the process-wide default.  Pass
    \c -1 to @ref Qore::Program::setJitOptimizationLevel()
    "Program::setJitOptimizationLevel()" to clear a %Program's override so that
    the process-wide default applies again.

    @code{.py}
# compile this Program's functions with the most aggressive optimization
Program::setJitOptimizationLevel(3);
    @endcode

    @note An invalid or empty \c QORE_JIT_OPT_LEVEL value is ignored and the
    default level is used.

    @note This setting applies to the JIT tier only.  The \c qcc AOT compiler has
    its own optimization level, set with its \c -O / \c --opt-level option and
    defaulting to \c 3; see @ref qcc_tool.

    @subsection jit_size_budget JIT Native Compilation Size Budget

    Native compilation of an exceptionally large function costs far more than it
    returns: the @ref exec_mode_ir "IR tier" already executes it, while LLVM's
    compilation cost grows superlinearly with function size.  Such a function can
    also exhaust the background compiler thread's stack (LLVM's ScalarEvolution
    analysis recurses on the function's control flow, and threads have small
    default stacks on some platforms), delay %Program destruction, which drains
    that program's pending compilations, or exhaust the memory of a constrained
    container.

    A function whose %Qore IR is larger than the native compilation budget
    therefore stays on the IR tier, where it executes normally.  The budget
    applies to every native compilation: functions and methods, top-level code,
    and interprocedural batches; an oversized batch
    is reduced to its root function, which is compiled with its callees left
    behind the normal runtime-call boundary.

    The budget is 10000 IR instructions by default and is set with the
    \c QORE_JIT_MAX_IR_INSTRUCTIONS environment variable; \c 0 means "no limit".
    An invalid or empty value is ignored and the default is used.

    @code{.sh}
# raise the budget for a build that natively compiles very large functions
QORE_JIT_MAX_IR_INSTRUCTIONS=50000 qore --exec-mode=jit script.qr
    @endcode

    @note Setting this variable to \c 0 or to a very large value restores the
    unbounded behavior and can crash or stall native compilation of pathologically
    large functions; raise it only when the platform's thread stack and memory are
    known to be sufficient.

    @note Set \c QORE_JIT_TIMING to report each skipped native compilation along
    with the function's IR instruction count and the budget in effect.

    @section exec_mode_tiered Tiered Execution Mode

    Tiered mode is the default for @ref modern "%modern" code and can also be
    selected explicitly with <tt>--exec-mode=tiered</tt>. Modern code is
    validated for IR lowerability when parsing is committed; lowering gaps are
    reported as parse-time errors because \c %%modern is the contract for
    optimized execution. Function bodies start with cached IR and can promote to
    JIT:

    - IR -> JIT after <tt>--jit-jit-threshold=N</tt> calls, default \c 1000

    @code{.sh}
qore --exec-mode=tiered script.qr
qore --exec-mode=tiered --jit-ir-threshold=50 --jit-jit-threshold=500 script.qr
    @endcode

    Promotion is non-blocking: native compilation is queued on a background
    thread and the function keeps executing through the IR interpreter until its
    compiled form is ready, after which subsequent calls run native.

    If native compilation fails for a function variant, that variant remains at
    IR instead of repeatedly retrying. IR lowering failures in modern code are
    treated as implementation bugs and reported during parse commit.

    @section exec_mode_aot AOT Compilation

    AOT compilation is provided by the @ref qcc_tool "qcc" tool. AOT compiles
    @ref modern "%modern" Qore code before deployment, eliminating parse-time
    overhead and allowing source text to be omitted from produced artifacts.

    AOT artifacts still require a compatible \c libqore at runtime. Each artifact
    carries QORD metadata with format version, feature flags, maximum IR opcode,
    dependencies, and optional diagnostic build information.

    Unlike direct script execution with \c qore, a generated AOT executable
    dispatches a named Qore entry function. The default entry is \c main(); use
    \c -e \c FN or \c --entry=FN to select another function. Code intended to
    start the application must therefore be inside that entry function;
    top-level executable statements are not a substitute for it. If the selected
    entry function is absent, the executable exits with a \c NO-FUNCTION
    exception.

    @subsection aot_artifacts Artifact Types

    |!Artifact|!Command|!Description
    |Executable|<tt>qcc -o app script.qr</tt>|Native launcher for one script
    |Executable from sources|<tt>qcc -o app lib.qr main.qr</tt>|One-shot native launcher for multiple script/application sources
    |Executable from objects|<tt>qcc -o app *.qo</tt>|Generated host that registers linked Qore objects
    |\c .qmod|<tt>qcc -m Module.qm</tt> or <tt>qcc -m qlib/Module</tt>|Binary user module
    |\c .qo|<tt>qcc -c file.qr</tt>|Relocatable object with native code and AOT metadata
    |\c .qoa|<tt>qcc -a --context=DIR *.qo</tt>|Static archive for C/C++ hosts

    Multi-file applications can be compiled directly to an executable:

    @code{.sh}
qcc -o app src/lib.qr src/main.qr src/worker.qr
    @endcode

    Build systems should normally use the explicit two-step form, compiling
    modern script/application sources to \c .qo objects, then linking those
    objects:

    @code{.sh}
qcc -c --output-dir=build/aot src/lib.qr src/main.qr src/worker.qr
qcc -o app build/aot/*.qo
    @endcode

    Build integrations can add \c --batch-build-sidecars and
    <tt>--depfile-dir=build/aot</tt> to the batch \c -c command. This emits the
    index, manifest, status, dependency, success-stamp, and content-stamp
    sidecars needed to seed later per-file incremental builds from the one
    shared parse.

    For incremental per-file builds, use \c -L to preload sibling \c .qo
    declarations before parsing the changed source:

    @code{.sh}
qcc -c -L build/aot --output-dir=build/aot src/worker.qr
qcc -o app build/aot/*.qo
    @endcode

    Each \c .qo object is written atomically: \c qcc compiles to a temporary
    file in the output directory and renames it into place on success.  A
    concurrent \c -L sibling scan therefore only ever observes a complete
    object, never a half-written one, so independent per-file \c qcc \c -c
    jobs writing into the same output directory may run in parallel (for
    example under <tt>make -j</tt> or \c ninja) without an external lock.

    @subsection aot_modules AOT-Compiled User Modules

    Standard user modules are compiled to \c .qmod during the CMake build when
    \c QORE_BUILD_AOT_MODULES is enabled. This option defaults to \c ON.

    Installed \c .qmod files are placed in the architecture-specific module
    directory.  Source \c .qm modules remain installed in the user-module source
    directory.  Module lookup checks compiled module directories before the
    corresponding source module directories, so installed user modules run
    through AOT by default while source modules remain available for
    documentation and development.

    Disable this behavior at configure time if needed:

    @code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build -DQORE_BUILD_AOT_MODULES=OFF
    @endcode

    @subsection aot_source_visibility Source Visibility

    \c qcc defaults to stripped source. Use \c --include-source when
    source-rich diagnostics or source inspection are required.
    Source-stripped artifacts contain QORD metadata and native code but not
    readable Qore source text. Missing AOT metadata is an error; source is not
    used as a runtime fallback for incomplete artifacts.

    @subsection aot_exception_locations Exception Source Locations

    Exceptions raised from AOT-compiled code report the real source file and
    line number for each Qore stack frame, matching interpreted execution. The
    throwing location and the full backtrace are reconstructed lazily, only when
    an exception is thrown, from line-number tables embedded in the artifact, so
    code that runs without throwing pays no per-statement location-tracking cost.

    Lazy reconstruction relies on the line tables emitted by default alongside
    debug info (\c -g). Compiling with \c --strip-debug-info removes those
    tables; such artifacts instead keep per-statement location tracking so source
    locations remain available without the embedded tables. Source text itself is
    not required: stripped-source artifacts still report file and line because
    those come from the line tables, not from embedded Qore source.

    @subsection qcc_tool Qore Code Compiler (qcc)

    Common \c qcc options:

    |!Option|!Description
    |\c -o, \c --output=FILE|Output file path
    |\c -O, \c --opt-level=N|LLVM optimization level 0-3, default 3
    |\c -m, \c --module|Compile a user module to \c .qmod
    |\c -c, \c --compile-only|Compile to a \c .qo relocatable object
    |\c --context=DIR|Context directory for split-module or per-file object compilation
    |\c -L DIR|Preload sibling \c .qo declarations for script-context compilation
    |\c --output-dir=DIR|Output directory for batch \c -c compilation
    |\c --depfile-dir=DIR|Write one dependency file per batch output into \c DIR
    |\c --batch-build-sidecars|Write incremental-build sidecars for every batch output; requires \c --depfile-dir
    |\c --batch-script-aggregate=SYM|Also emit runtime aggregate metadata from the committed batch program, avoiding a second parse; requires \c --batch-script-aggregate-output
    |\c --batch-script-aggregate-output=FILE|Output path for the fused batch runtime aggregate
    |\c --batch-script-aggregate-manifest-input=FILE|Record a fused-aggregate manifest input; repeat to match a standalone aggregate rule
    |\c -l, \c --load=MOD|Load a module into the compile program before parsing
    |\c --stub=FILE|Preload declaration-only Qore source supplied by a host
    |\c --define=NAME[=VALUE]|Set a parser define before parsing
    |\c --parse-option=NAME|OR a parse option into the compile program
    |\c --script-aggregate-native-registers|Bind aggregate metadata to the linked per-file native bodies; valid with standalone or fused batch script aggregates. Unless \c --include-source is given, the aggregate then carries declarations only and lowers no code bodies of its own, so it is reported as emitting declaration metadata rather than a code-variant count
    |\c --depfile=FILE|Write a Make-format dependency file
    |\c --depfile-module-deps=MODE|For module depfiles, record dependency sources (default), resolved artifact files, or none; source mode expands split modules to all \c .qm / \c .qc / \c .ql files and retains an artifact only when no source representation is available
    |\c --from-objects|Aggregate \c .qo inputs into a \c .qmod with \c -m
    |\c -a, \c --archive|Create a \c .qoa archive from \c .qo inputs
    |\c -S, \c --static|Static link single-source executables against \c libqore
    |\c -t, \c --target=TRIPLE|Cross-compilation target triple
    |\c --include-source|Embed Qore source text
    |\c --strip-source|Omit Qore source text
    |\c --strip-debug-info|Omit DWARF debug info
    |\c -g|Emit DWARF debug info
    |\c --time-trace[=PATH]|Emit Chrome-format LLVM timing trace
    |\c --big-fn-threshold=N|Use \c OptimizeNone for functions with at least N IR blocks, default 200
    |\c --jobs=N|Parallel backend-codegen threads (split-after-opt); default CPU count, throttled by the GNU make jobserver, \c 1 = single-object
    |\c -e, \c --entry=FN|Entry function for generated one-shot or link-mode \c main()
    |\c -r, \c --warnings-are-errors|Treat Qore parser warnings as fatal compile errors
    |\c --dump-info|Inspect embedded AOT metadata without executing code
    |\c --dump-symbols|Include an nm-like symbol table in dump output
    |\c --dump-sections|Include object and AOT section tables in dump output

    @subsection qcc_parallel_codegen Parallel Backend Codegen and the GNU make Jobserver

    \c qcc can parallelize LLVM backend codegen for a single compilation using a
    <i>split-after-opt</i> strategy: it optimizes the whole module once, then partitions
    the optimized module along whole-function boundaries, runs codegen for the partitions
    concurrently, and links the results back into one relocatable object that is
    symbol-equivalent and byte-deterministic with respect to the single-object build.

    - \c --jobs=N sets the number of codegen threads (default: CPU count, capped at 32;
      \c 1 selects the single-object reference path). The equivalent environment variable
      is \c QCC_JOBS (see @ref environment_variables); an explicit \c --jobs flag wins.
    - Only modules at or above \c QCC_SPLIT_THRESHOLD optimized-IR instructions (default
      \c 50000) are split, so small modules keep the single-object path.

    <b>GNU make jobserver integration.</b> When \c qcc runs as part of a parallel
    <tt>make -jN</tt> build (for example the CMake user-module build, or a module or host
    project that compiles \c .qo files), it participates as a
    <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/make/manual/html_node/Job-Slots.html">GNU make
    jobserver</a> client so that \c qcc's internal codegen threads and make's own parallel
    recipes share one global job budget rather than oversubscribing the machine. \c qcc
    reads \c MAKEFLAGS and supports both the make 4.4+ named-pipe form
    (<tt>--jobserver-auth=fifo:PATH</tt>) and the older inherited-file-descriptor form
    (<tt>--jobserver-auth=R,W</tt> / <tt>--jobserver-fds=R,W</tt>). Each qcc process owns
    one implicit job slot; additional codegen concurrency is used only for as many extra
    jobserver tokens as it can acquire non-blockingly <i>before</i> splitting. If no spare
    tokens are available (a saturated build), \c qcc falls back to single-object codegen,
    so parallel codegen is safe to leave enabled by default even under <tt>make -j</tt>.
    Outside a jobserver-aware make (a bare \c qcc invocation), \c --jobs / \c QCC_JOBS
    apply directly.

    @subsection aot_inspection Inspecting AOT Artifacts

    Use \c qcc \c --dump-info to inspect AOT metadata in executables, \c .qmod
    modules, \c .qo fragments, \c .qoa archives, or ordinary object files:

    @code{.sh}
qcc --dump-info MyModule.qmod
qcc --dump-info --dump-symbols app
qcc --dump-info --dump-sections build/file.qo
    @endcode

    Inspection does not execute embedded Qore code.

    @section exec_mode_programmatic Programmatic Control

    Execution mode can be controlled through @ref Qore::Program "Program":

    @code{.py}
%modern

Program pgm(PO_MODERN);
pgm.setExecMode(ExecMode::EM_JIT);
pgm.parse("int sub compute() { return 42; }", "code");
int result = pgm.callFunction("compute");
    @endcode

    @subsection exec_mode_enum ExecMode Enum

    |!Value|!Description
    |@ref Qore::ExecMode::EM_AST "EM_AST"|AST interpreter
    |@ref Qore::ExecMode::EM_IR "EM_IR"|IR bytecode interpreter
    |@ref Qore::ExecMode::EM_JIT "EM_JIT"|LLVM JIT compilation
    |@ref Qore::ExecMode::EM_TIERED "EM_TIERED"|Adaptive tiered execution

    @section exec_mode_requirements Requirements

    IR, JIT, tiered execution, and AOT require @ref modern "%modern" code.
    Files whose extension is not \c .q, including \c .qr, enable modern mode by
    default; \c .q remains legacy by default for compatibility.

    LLVM 16 or later is required for JIT and AOT support.

    @see
    - @ref command_line_processing "Command-line processing"
    - @ref qore_modules "Qore modules"
    - @ref modern "%modern parse directive"
*/
